#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092091 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092239 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf389905-8964-57b6-85bc-2fd738f05f11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of the Region Bounded by Two Polar Curves With Multiple Intersections"}] :question/id #uuid "8a885f78-0028-5b6e-80e6-1215f5d934a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-639" :question/problem "Find the **total** area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=2\\cos(2\\theta)$ and $r=1$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c023033b4d3e0fa6ea5a2d9866688e360b759fba6aa0a5dd6241e148d7b093d.png)" :question/worked-solution "Consider the rightmost pair of petals. First, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos (2θ) &= 1 \\\\\n\\cos (2θ) &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$:\n\n$$\n2\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/1d/1dc9ce9add3d7135f69c92590ebde003d4e5ee31446d6202222ac890e2f42a9c.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1$, and\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\cos(2\\theta)$.\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big(\\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{1} &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{- π/6}^{π/6}((2\\cos (2θ))^{2} - 1^{2})dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{- π/6}^{π/6}(4\\cos^{2} (2θ) - 1)dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{- π/6}^{π/6}(2(1 + \\cos (4θ)) - 1)dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{- π/6}^{π/6}(1 + 2\\cos (4θ))dθ \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{2}θ\\right|_{- π/6}^{π/6} + \\left.\\frac{1}{4}\\sin (4θ)\\right|_{- π/6}^{π/6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{π}{6} - (-\\frac{π}{6})) + \\frac{1}{4}(\\sin \\frac{2π}{3} - \\sin (-\\frac{2π}{3})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6} + \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} - (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= 4A_{1} = 4(\\frac{π}{6} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}) = \\frac{2π}{3} + \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092092 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3361ca6a-66d4-515b-b529-c433a85b7a96" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of the Region Bounded by Two Polar Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "c30d91c2-ee63-5b20-ab39-6d356f711fd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-640" :question/problem "Find the shaded area that lies between the polar curves $r=\\sin\\theta$ and $r=2\\sin\\theta$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/23/2389273b09df3e9bc9262a2daed848fbd518707e683e5d06a8dd0217766036bb.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sin θ &= \\sin θ \\\\\n\\sin θ &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for\n\n$$\n\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\n\\theta = 0, \\pi\n$$\n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/4c/4ce437c0d6b1f196269c5c1e56e687a6dca21fd407834dce20bfa7ac76298002.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=\\sin\\theta$, and\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\sin\\theta$.\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big(\\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\theta_1=0, \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad \\theta_2=\\pi\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{π}((2\\sin θ)^{2} - (\\sin θ)^{2})dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{π}3\\sin^{2} θdθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}\\int_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos 2θ)dθ \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{3}{4}(θ - \\frac{\\sin 2θ}{2})\\right|_{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}[(π - (\\sin (2π))/(2)) - (0 - \\frac{\\sin 0}{2})] \\\\\n&= \\frac{3π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092123 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092277 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01741ca-0ee3-554c-a2c2-788c97d040fd" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Integral Expression For the Area Bounded by Two Polar Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "dd29a0e8-2912-531e-a44c-62aa6d604273" :question/math-academy-id "e-7361" :question/problem "Find an integral expression that gives the shaded area between the polar curves $r=2\\cos(3\\theta)$ and $r=1$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fdeb4d842f141d702027e7a2ec3e7618eacd57c3c97119f851dd2497d8ee813.png)" :question/worked-solution "Consider the rightmost petal, oriented along the $x$-axis. First, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos (3θ) &= 1 \\\\\n\\cos (3θ) &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for\n\n$$\n3\\theta \\in \\left[-\\pi,\\pi\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\n3\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{9}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{9}\n$$\n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/89/8950c8f5e8ea54736714e83f584ccc4b164cdee53cf3c3f64a8e0c9f81f16e6a.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1$, and\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\cos(3\\theta)$.\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big(\\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{9}, \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{- π/9}^{π/9}[(2\\cos (3θ))^{2} - 1^{2}]dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{- π/9}^{π/9}(4\\cos^{2} (3θ) - 1)dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092124 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3971880e-a648-58f0-ad82-d87833d62169" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Integral Expression For the Area Bounded by Two Polar Curves When the Intersections Are Given"}] :question/id #uuid "4bc28849-2812-5b53-a2da-36198c06e290" :question/math-academy-id "e-7362" :question/problem "Find an integral expression that gives the area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=\\cos\\theta$ and $r=\\sin\\theta$, as shown below.\n\n*Hint: In the first quadrant, the curves intersect when $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.*\n\n![](images/7f/7fcd600ef6c13f5c643ffa929fa44f5dfa683f9af5555ac97ed5540a9f09d9b3.png)" :question/worked-solution "We are given that our curves intersect when\n\n$$\n\\theta_1= \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/7f/7f016c0657ffc3566d32248d9fcb62bbb4cea73777188b7bf7843e93feb16f88.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=\\cos\\theta$, and\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=\\sin\\theta$.\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big(\\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\theta_1= \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}, \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{π/4}^{π/2}(\\sin θ)^{2} - (\\cos θ)^{2}dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{π/4}^{π/2}(\\sin^{2} θ - \\cos^{2} θ)dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{π/4}^{π/2} - \\cos 2θdθ \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{π/4}^{π/2}\\cos 2θdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092277 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01741ca-0ee3-554c-a2c2-788c97d040fd" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Integral Expression For the Area Bounded by Two Polar Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf20c715-06c2-53f1-ad83-e2eaf6717880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{π/6}^{5π/6}(2 - 2\\sin (2θ))dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090791} {:answer/id #uuid "0780cd2e-87bd-5c34-98c3-abdd801f139a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/3}^{π/3}(1 + 2\\cos (2θ))dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090792} {:answer/id #uuid "0a01dc2f-c260-5985-b0de-fd31b4da23e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}(2 + 2\\sin (2θ))dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090793} {:answer/id #uuid "346558ef-9a66-5556-a038-e16d5e7d5d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{π/6}^{5π/6}(1 + 2\\cos (2θ))dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090794} {:answer/id #uuid "61231e2d-5078-595d-8d8d-650aefccb67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/3}^{π/2}(1 - \\cos (2θ))dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090795}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f7de1137-af83-5dbc-a6d6-01c665e55f57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090790}] :question/id #uuid "223cc6ef-68a5-5f2e-83a6-238009c2a928" :question/math-academy-id "q-51268" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2db9a6bb859b74c6755dc79261372f58c87b8378d4acc997cad819c32d5faf18.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r = 2\\cos θ$ and $r = 1$, as shown above?"}]}
